{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 10, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Tripoli and the last ruler of the County of Tripoli. He was born in 1152 and died in 1187. Raymond III was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch and his wife Orgueilleuse of Harenc.\n\n* **Early Life** *\nRaymond III was raised in the Byzantine Empire where his father Bohemond III had taken refuge after losing the County of Tripoli to the Muslim forces led by Nur ad-Din in 1164. He was educated in the Byzantine court and later joined the army of the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Komnenos.\n\nIn 1173 Raymond III and his father Bohemond III led a successful campaign against the County of Tripoli which was then under Muslim control. They were able to retake the county and Raymond III became the Count of Tripoli in 1173. However his reign was marked by constant conflict with the Muslim forces led by Saladin.\n\n* **Military Campaigns** *\nRaymond III played a key role in the Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader forces were defeated by the Muslim forces led by Saladin. He was one of the few survivors of the battle and was taken prisoner by Saladin. Raymond III was later released in 1191 in exchange for 16000 dinars.\n\nRaymond III's reign was also marked by his attempts to unite the Crusader states against the Muslim forces. He formed an alliance with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Principality of Antioch to counter the growing power of the Muslim forces. However his efforts were ultimately unsuccessful and the County of Tripoli was conquered by the Muslim forces in 1188.\n\n* **Family and Succession** *\nRaymond III married a woman named Eschiva of Bures and had several children with her. However his marriage was childless and he was succeeded by his nephew Bohemond IV of Antioch who was the son of his brother Raymond IV of Antioch.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.014390614}, {"tok